WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.601 --> 00:00:04.003
-Welcome back to "Late Night."
We're here with Danny DeVito.

00:00:04.003 --> 00:00:05.971
You have some great guest voices
on the show...

00:00:05.971 --> 00:00:07.406
-Oh, yeah.

00:00:07.406 --> 00:00:09.108
-...including your "Twins"
costar Arnold Schwarzenegger.

00:00:09.108 --> 00:00:10.676
-Arnold Schwarzenegger.
Well, you know, when you're --

00:00:10.676 --> 00:00:13.279
You know, you're doing the show,
we're doing the --

00:00:13.279 --> 00:00:15.348
I asked everybody
that I thought --

00:00:15.348 --> 00:00:17.717
when a part came up that
I thought was right for them,

00:00:17.717 --> 00:00:21.887
and Arnold immediately said yes,
and that was --

00:00:21.887 --> 00:00:23.389
-What was the part
that made you --

00:00:23.389 --> 00:00:24.623
-He's an announcer.
-Okay.

00:00:24.623 --> 00:00:27.293
-And he's talking about
"the realm of realms!"

00:00:27.293 --> 00:00:30.096
And he's, like, got that voice,
you know, that anybody --

00:00:30.096 --> 00:00:31.965
You know, you hear that voice,
and it's just like,

00:00:31.965 --> 00:00:34.500
"Ah, it's Arnold.
It's Schwarzenegger."

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:36.402
-Did you know him
before "Twins"?

00:00:36.402 --> 00:00:37.803
Or did you --
-Uh, no.

00:00:37.803 --> 00:00:39.172
-Okay.
-No, I --

00:00:39.172 --> 00:00:41.440
The first time I met Arnold,
I was getting out of a car

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:44.010
with my kids in -- at a hotel.

00:00:44.010 --> 00:00:47.613
And I was full of all the bags,
you know, the carry-on --

00:00:47.613 --> 00:00:50.983
the kids' bags that you have to
have when you've got three kids.

00:00:50.983 --> 00:00:53.519
And I was coming out,
and somebody grabbed my arm

00:00:53.519 --> 00:00:57.223
as I was getting out of the car
because I was struggling.

00:00:57.223 --> 00:00:59.191
And I thought
it was the doorman.

00:00:59.191 --> 00:01:01.528
And I say, "Okay, thank you,"
and I got out of the car.

00:01:01.528 --> 00:01:02.862
And I turn around, it was --

00:01:02.862 --> 00:01:04.296
[as Arnold] "Any time, Danny."

00:01:04.296 --> 00:01:06.398
[Normal voice]
And it was Schwarzenegger.

00:01:06.398 --> 00:01:08.701
And so that was the first time
I met him.

00:01:08.701 --> 00:01:12.072
Then later on, we did -- you
know, then we got to do "Twins."

00:01:12.072 --> 00:01:14.274
And then another thing
that happened.

00:01:14.274 --> 00:01:17.843
There was a part in the show,
in "Little Demon,"

00:01:17.843 --> 00:01:19.579
that was a millipede.

00:01:19.579 --> 00:01:21.881
A millipede.
It's like this really weird --

00:01:21.881 --> 00:01:23.883
And it was a really great
little part.

00:01:23.883 --> 00:01:27.053
So, back up --
Mel Brooks is doing

00:01:27.053 --> 00:01:28.854
"History of the World: Part II."

00:01:28.854 --> 00:01:31.690
He asked me --
He's executive producer.

00:01:31.690 --> 00:01:34.927
He asked me to be in the show
playing Czar Nicholas.

00:01:34.927 --> 00:01:38.063
It was four or five lines.
I say, "Yeah, okay, I'll do it."

00:01:38.063 --> 00:01:40.700
But in the interest of --
Because I'm from New Jersey --

00:01:40.700 --> 00:01:42.067
In the interest of

00:01:42.067 --> 00:01:44.737
"I scratch your back,
you scratch my back" --

00:01:44.737 --> 00:01:47.340
So Mel Brooks is also
in "Little Demon."

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:48.541
-That's great.
-Yeah.

00:01:48.541 --> 00:01:50.243
-A little bit of horse trading
on the side.

00:01:50.243 --> 00:01:51.711
-A little bit of horse trading.
-Yeah.

00:01:51.711 --> 00:01:53.746
-You know, and Dave Bautista,
who I met -- You know Dave?

00:01:53.746 --> 00:01:55.214
-Yeah.
-Amazing guy.

00:01:55.214 --> 00:01:57.616
Yeah. You know, it's a lot of --
a lot of great people.

00:01:57.616 --> 00:02:00.119
And Rhea had to do it, too.
-Yeah, well, of course.

00:02:00.119 --> 00:02:01.821
-Rhea did it.
Yeah, she did, yes.

00:02:01.821 --> 00:02:05.424
-You -- We talked about --
We mentioned "Batman Returns."

00:02:05.424 --> 00:02:08.194
That's the 30-year anniversary
of that film.

00:02:08.194 --> 00:02:10.396
-Oh, boy.
-20 years, "Death to Smoochy."

00:02:10.396 --> 00:02:11.530
-Oh, my goodness.

00:02:11.530 --> 00:02:12.798
-Do you ever revisit
your old work?

00:02:12.798 --> 00:02:14.467
-Oh, oh, I -- once in a while.

00:02:14.467 --> 00:02:17.102
You know, every once in a while
I'll catch Oswald because

00:02:17.102 --> 00:02:20.206
he's on, you know -- he's
iconically all over the place.

00:02:20.206 --> 00:02:22.742
And I love "Death to Smoochy."

00:02:22.742 --> 00:02:24.443
So I -- I watch that --

00:02:24.443 --> 00:02:27.046
I've watched that
in the past, like, six months.

00:02:27.046 --> 00:02:28.414
Six months ago, I watched it.

00:02:28.414 --> 00:02:30.082
-And a film you directed,
as well.

00:02:30.082 --> 00:02:32.151
-Yes, I did.
Catherine Keener's in it.

00:02:32.151 --> 00:02:34.254
-Do you --
Do you love directing?

00:02:34.254 --> 00:02:35.421
-I do.

00:02:35.421 --> 00:02:36.822
-Yeah, you've done it
a fair amount.

00:02:36.822 --> 00:02:38.558
-Yeah, I've done it
a fair amount. Yeah, yeah.

00:02:38.558 --> 00:02:41.828
The first one was "Throw Momma."

00:02:41.828 --> 00:02:43.562
-Yeah.
-"Throw Momma from the Train."

00:02:43.562 --> 00:02:46.265
-Yeah. That was a fantastic
and iconic film.

00:02:46.265 --> 00:02:48.100
You got off -- You hit the
ground running as a director.

00:02:48.100 --> 00:02:49.969
-Yeah, I love that one.
Yeah, that was a good one.

00:02:49.969 --> 00:02:51.704
-You were the perfect person
for a film called

00:02:51.704 --> 00:02:53.907
"Throw Momma from the Train."
-Yeah.

00:02:53.907 --> 00:02:55.207
-We've talked about this before,

00:02:55.207 --> 00:02:56.675
but, you know,
I knew you as a kid.

00:02:56.675 --> 00:02:58.444
I grew up watching,
you know, "Taxi" reruns,

00:02:58.444 --> 00:02:59.879
and so I knew you from "Taxi."

00:02:59.879 --> 00:03:02.281
And then the first time I saw
"Cuckoo's Nest," I realized,

00:03:02.281 --> 00:03:03.816
"Oh, my God.
That's Danny DeVito."

00:03:03.816 --> 00:03:05.751
-Yeah, that's me.
-That's one of your first, yes?

00:03:05.751 --> 00:03:07.320
-That was one of
my first movies, yeah.

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:09.255
-What was your audition like?
What are your memories of --

00:03:09.255 --> 00:03:11.491
-Well, "Cuckoo's Nest"
was different

00:03:11.491 --> 00:03:13.993
because I was doing the play
off Broadway.

00:03:13.993 --> 00:03:16.095
So I was into the character.

00:03:16.095 --> 00:03:18.931
So I had months and months
to prepare.

00:03:18.931 --> 00:03:21.300
And then I met Milos,
and the rest is history.

00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:23.169
I did the play.
I mean, the movie.

00:03:23.169 --> 00:03:24.837
And it was, you know, a natural.

00:03:24.837 --> 00:03:27.239
I had already done
a lot of the work.

00:03:27.239 --> 00:03:29.441
"Taxi," of course --

00:03:29.441 --> 00:03:34.380
My famous "Taxi" intro was I --

00:03:34.380 --> 00:03:38.517
I was given the script
by a casting director.

00:03:38.517 --> 00:03:40.086
This is the way
it usually works.

00:03:40.086 --> 00:03:42.955
You get the script, and then
you have to go in and audition.

00:03:42.955 --> 00:03:45.991
And I went in with the script.
Nobody knew me.

00:03:45.991 --> 00:03:48.427
And all these people
are around the room.

00:03:48.427 --> 00:03:50.563
All the writers.
All the producers.

00:03:50.563 --> 00:03:52.231
And you've got to audition.

00:03:52.231 --> 00:03:55.268
And I said, "One thing
I want to know before we start.

00:03:55.268 --> 00:03:57.570
Who wrote this [bleep]?"
And I threw it on the table.

00:03:57.570 --> 00:03:59.037
[ Laughter ]

00:03:59.037 --> 00:04:01.307
And that was like Louie
walking into their --

00:04:01.307 --> 00:04:02.775
-Yeah.
-You know, their -- Yeah.

00:04:02.775 --> 00:04:05.345
So that was -- that was that.

00:04:05.345 --> 00:04:08.347
And, you know, you audition
for many, many --

00:04:08.347 --> 00:04:09.782
-Yeah.
-You know.

00:04:09.782 --> 00:04:11.450
-I would assume it was obviously
a little bit different

00:04:11.450 --> 00:04:13.419
when you come around
to "It's Always Sunny."

00:04:13.419 --> 00:04:16.689
But I -- You can't have imagined
when you started doing that show

00:04:16.689 --> 00:04:18.224
that it would be on
for 16 seasons.

00:04:18.224 --> 00:04:19.859
-No.
-I mean, what an accomplishment.

00:04:19.859 --> 00:04:23.629
-No, but, you know, I saw --
John Landgraf was at FX.

00:04:23.629 --> 00:04:27.032
He sent me eight episodes of
this show that had been done

00:04:27.032 --> 00:04:28.601
that was "Always Sunny."

00:04:28.601 --> 00:04:32.171
The guys did it. Charlie and
Glenn and Rob and Kaitlin.

00:04:32.171 --> 00:04:34.740
And I thought it was great.

00:04:34.740 --> 00:04:38.144
And then a while went by,
and I just said, you know --

00:04:38.144 --> 00:04:39.812
I just gave him my opinion
of the show,

00:04:39.812 --> 00:04:41.780
that it was -- I thought
it was a terrific show.

00:04:41.780 --> 00:04:44.016
And then like a few months
went by, and they said,

00:04:44.016 --> 00:04:46.252
"Would you consider
being on it?"

00:04:46.252 --> 00:04:47.686
And I said,
well, if they write a --

00:04:47.686 --> 00:04:50.756
you know, a part that was,
like, organic in the show,

00:04:50.756 --> 00:04:52.558
that I was, you know,
in a good --

00:04:52.558 --> 00:04:54.594
that was funny and all of this.

00:04:54.594 --> 00:04:57.963
And then I said, "Well, what --
what part could I play

00:04:57.963 --> 00:05:00.466
on that show that would
really be believable?"

00:05:00.466 --> 00:05:05.571
And I'm Kaitlin
and Dennis' dad!

00:05:05.571 --> 00:05:07.106
It was like
the most ridiculous --

00:05:07.106 --> 00:05:09.942
These two tall blond kids.
You know?

00:05:09.942 --> 00:05:12.411
And I said, "Well, you know,
how's that going to work?"

00:05:12.411 --> 00:05:14.046
And I said, "Well,
the only way it gotta work

00:05:14.046 --> 00:05:16.516
is that my wife was a whore."

00:05:16.516 --> 00:05:19.852
That's all it is.
And it did work.

00:05:19.852 --> 00:05:21.553
You know, kinda, you know --
-Yeah.

00:05:21.553 --> 00:05:23.556
-It fit in.
-Yeah, it's very nice.

00:05:23.556 --> 00:05:25.625
Very good work
of fixing the script.

00:05:25.625 --> 00:05:28.427
-Yeah, you want to get in there,
make sure it's believable.

00:05:28.427 --> 00:05:30.028
-Yeah.
When I watch "Always Sunny,"

00:05:30.028 --> 00:05:31.797
the thing that I want
more than anything

00:05:31.797 --> 00:05:33.832
is authenticity
and believability.

00:05:33.832 --> 00:05:35.233
-Yeah, absolutely.
-Yeah.

00:05:35.233 --> 00:05:36.636
-You want to be grounded.

00:05:36.636 --> 00:05:38.237
-You want to be grounded
when I watch "Sunny."

00:05:38.237 --> 00:05:40.339
Hey, it's always a pleasure
to see you.

00:05:40.339 --> 00:05:43.242
Congrats on the new show to you,
your daughter, your son.

00:05:43.242 --> 00:05:45.078
Danny DeVito.

00:05:45.078 --> 00:05:47.613
"Little Demon"
airs Thursdays on FXX

00:05:47.613 --> 00:05:49.916
and is available to stream
the next day on Hulu.

